    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Pyrenean kingdom that took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> in the twelfth century.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronilla-of-aragon">Petronila</a>'s marriage to the count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> created the Crown of Aragon, which later joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</li><li>Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a>, fueros and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justicia-de-aragon">Justicia</a> limited royal power until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V</a> abolished them in 1707.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-ii-of-aragon">Ramiro II</a>, a monk called from the cloister to the throne in 1134, married to provide an heir and retired to monastic life once his baby daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronilla-of-aragon">Petronila</a> was betrothed.</p></aside>
    </section>

