    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/aznar-galindez-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aznar-galindez-i.jpg" alt="Aznar Galíndez I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/aznar-galindez-i">Aznar Galíndez I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aznar_I_Gal%C3%ADndez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Autor desconhecido, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aragon took its name from a river in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, where a small county around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaca">Jaca</a> appeared in the early ninth century under Frankish influence, ruled by counts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aznar-galindez-i">Aznar Galíndez</a>. In the tenth century it passed by marriage to the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>). When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III the Great of Navarre</a> died in 1035, his illegitimate son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-garces-of-viguera">Ramiro</a> I received Aragon. Ramiro never formally took the royal title, but he ruled as an independent king, and in the mid-1040s he absorbed the neighbouring counties of Sobrarbe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a>. He died in 1063 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-graus">Graus</a>, fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, whose ruler was supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ramirez">Sancho Ramírez</a> (r. 1063–1094) placed the kingdom under papal protection in 1068, introduced the Roman liturgy in 1071 and became king of Navarre as well in 1076, after his cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iv-of-castile">Sancho IV</a> was murdered. He was killed besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a>, which his son Peter I took after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alcoraz">Battle of Alcoraz</a> in 1096; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbastro">Barbastro</a> followed in 1100. The decisive expansion came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I</a> the Battler (r. 1104–1134), who captured Zaragoza, the great Muslim city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley, in December 1118 and made it his capital, and then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarazona">Tarazona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daroca">Daroca</a>, more than doubling the kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/teruel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teruel.jpg" alt="Teruel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/teruel">Teruel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plaza_del_Torico_(Teruel).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Luis Mieza, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom joined two contrasting worlds: the mountain valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, with monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monastery-of-san-juan-de-la-pena">San Juan de la Peña</a> and a pastoral economy, and the irrigated lowlands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley conquered in the twelfth century. Unlike many conquerors, the kings let much of the Muslim population stay on the land, and the <em>mudéjares</em>, Muslims living under Christian rule, remained a large part of the rural workforce on the estates of nobles and the military orders, especially in the Ebro and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalon">Jalón</a> valleys. Their builders created the brick <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjar</a> architecture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teruel">Teruel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calatayud">Calatayud</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. Jewish communities lived in Zaragoza, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> and other towns until the expulsion of 1492. Christian settlers were drawn to the frontier by generous charters such as the fuero of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaca">Jaca</a>, which became a model for towns along the pilgrim road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>. Wheat, wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> and especially wool from transhumant flocks were the main products, and Zaragoza became the commercial and political centre. Aragon was poorer and more rural than Catalonia, and its trade depended largely on Catalan and Valencian ports. The forced conversion of the remaining Muslims in 1525–1526 and the expulsion of their descendants, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a>, in 1610 removed tens of thousands of farmers and badly damaged the countryside.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cutanda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cutanda.jpg" alt="Battle of Cutanda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cutanda">Battle of Cutanda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cutanda.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dv.uzzi, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Aragon's early armies were small bands of mountain nobles and their followers, but the kings learned to besiege fortified cities and drew help from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ramirez">Sancho Ramírez</a> and Peter I fought a long war of sieges in the foothills, and the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbastro">Barbastro</a> in 1064 by an expedition of French and Norman knights, lost again the next year, anticipated the crusading ideal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I</a> the Battler was the great soldier of the dynasty. Aided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaston-iv-of-bearn">Gaston IV of Béarn</a> and other southern French lords, he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> after a siege of about seven months in 1118, defeated an Almoravid army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cutanda">Battle of Cutanda</a> in 1120 and in 1125–1126 led a raid deep into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, returning with thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozarabs">Mozarab Christians</a> to settle his new lands. He died in 1134 soon after his defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fraga">Battle of Fraga</a>. To hold the frontier, kings relied on the military orders, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Hospitallers</a>, and on town militias raised under the fueros, and Alfonso II founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teruel">Teruel</a> around 1170 to guard the south. After the union with Catalonia the kingdom's forces fought in the wars of the Crown of Aragon: Peter II died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muret">Battle of Muret</a> in 1213, and James I the Conqueror led Aragonese knights in the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a> from 1229 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, which fell in 1238.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon.jpg" alt="Peter III of Aragon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III of Aragon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pedro_III_rey_de_Arag%C3%B3n.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Villani, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom developed some of the strongest constitutional checks on monarchy in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a>, unusually, had four estates or ‘arms’: the high nobility (<em>ricoshombres</em>), the lesser nobles and knights, the clergy and the towns. In 1247 James I had the kingdom's customary laws compiled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> as the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fueros-de-aragon">Fueros de Aragón</a></em>. In 1283, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-aragon">Peter III</a> was at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, nobles and towns organised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-aragon">Union of Aragon</a> forced him to grant the General Privilege, and in 1287 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-aragon">Alfonso III</a> yielded the still wider Privileges of the Union, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iv-of-aragon">Peter IV</a> crushed the Union at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-epila">Battle of Épila</a> in 1348. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justicia-de-aragon">Justicia de Aragón</a>, a judge chosen from the knights, decided disputes between the king and the nobility and could take people and property under his protection against royal officials, and in the fifteenth century the office became in practice irremovable. Kings had to swear to observe the fueros before receiving homage. The later legend that the Aragonese swore loyalty to their king only ‘if not, not’, that is, only as long as he kept their laws, was a sixteenth-century invention, but it reflects a real tradition of contractual kingship. After 1479 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroys</a> represented the absent kings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cortes">Cortes</a> and the Justicia went on guarding the kingdom's privileges.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/barcelona"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/barcelona.jpg" alt="Barcelona" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evening_light_over_Barcelona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">M McBey, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The marriage that made Aragon part of a larger state had lasting results for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I</a>'s death, his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-ii-of-aragon">Ramiro II</a>, a monk recalled from the cloister to rule, fathered a daughter, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronilla-of-aragon">Petronila</a>, and in 1137 betrothed her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer IV</a>, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>. Their son Alfonso II, who reigned from 1164, was both king of Aragon and count of Barcelona, and the resulting Crown of Aragon, a union of separate realms each keeping its own laws, became a major Mediterranean power that later took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. Aragon itself gave the Crown its royal title and its name. The marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II of Aragon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isabella-i-of-castile">Isabella I of Castile</a> in 1469 then joined the Crowns of Aragon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> under the Catholic Monarchs, the foundation of the Spanish monarchy. The Aragonese model of a composite monarchy, in which a common king ruled kingdoms that kept their own laws, assemblies and coinage, shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> Spain. Aragon's legal tradition still survives: its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>, restored after 1711, is in force today in the autonomous community of Aragon, which has revived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justicia-de-aragon">Justicia</a> as an ombudsman. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjar</a> monuments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teruel">Teruel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> are listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO</a> as World Heritage.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-almansa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-almansa.jpg" alt="Battle of Almansa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-almansa">Battle of Almansa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balaca-Battle_of_Almansa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Within the Crown of Aragon the kingdom gradually lost weight to Catalonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, which were richer and more populous, and after the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1479 its kings lived far away. When the last king of the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-aragon">Martin the Humane</a>, died in 1410, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compromise-of-caspe">Compromise of Caspe</a> in 1412 gave the throne to a Castilian prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-aragon">Ferdinand of Antequera</a>. In 1591 the crown's attempt to remove Philip II's fugitive secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-perez">Antonio Pérez</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justicia-de-aragon">Justicia</a>'s prison provoked riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>; a royal army entered the kingdom, the Justicia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-lanuza-y-urrea">Juan de Lanuza</a> was executed without trial, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarazona">Tarazona</a> in 1592 curtailed the fueros. The expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a> in 1610 further impoverished the countryside. The end came in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. In 1705–1706 much of Aragon, with Catalonia and Valencia, declared for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> claimant Archduke Charles against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V</a>. After the Bourbon victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-almansa">Battle of Almansa</a> on 25 April 1707, Philip V abolished the fueros of Aragon and Valencia by right of conquest in a decree of 29 June 1707, the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nueva-planta-decrees">Nueva Planta decrees</a>. Aragon was governed thereafter on the Castilian model, with a captain-general and an <em>audiencia</em>, as a province of the unified Bourbon kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Aragon grew from a Pyrenean county into the conqueror of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley, gave its name to the Crown of Aragon and, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II</a>, helped create the Spanish monarchy, while its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a>, fueros and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justicia-de-aragon">Justicia</a> formed one of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s strongest traditions of limits on royal power.</em></p>

